+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration string into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a bare
+integer number of seconds, or one or more `<number><unit>` parts with units `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, and `ms`, such as `90s`,
+`1h30m`, or `1m500ms`. Each unit may appear at most once, largest first, with no separators. Negative values are
+rejected.
+
+`min` and `max` are inclusive bounds, given as `timedelta` objects. If either is omitted, that side is unbounded.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    from datetime import timedelta
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option(
+        "--timeout",
+        type=click.Duration(min=timedelta(seconds=30), max=timedelta(hours=1)),
+    )
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"waiting {timeout.total_seconds()} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1m500ms'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=2h'])
+```
+
